                "text": "**Tracing the Path**\n\nI'm currently focused on charting the path of the \"3\"s. My process started at a \"3\" at row 'r', column 0, moving right, and now I am mapping its interaction with \"8\"s and \"6\"s to understand the reflections. I am looking for the reflection characteristics of each pixel type.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Interactions**\n\nI've been analyzing the reflections. I now understand that \"8\"s reflect right-moving rays upwards, while \"6\"s reflect them downwards. I'm testing this with the reflection of a ray that is coming from up. The initial findings now are that the ray changes direction to right. I am trying to determine what pattern or characteristics within the data caused the change.\n\n\n**Revisiting Reflection Rules**\n\nI am now revisiting the reflection rules, specifically focusing on how \"8\"s behave. I've realized that my initial slash (\"/\") mirror analogy needs refinement. Specifically, I was incorrect in assuming the '/' applies in screen coordinates. Considering movement from the top, I saw an 8 reflected the ray right, not left! I am trying to determine the correct rule in the current orientation. I need to make sure I am not thinking about reflection characteristics incorrectly.\n\n\n**Clarifying Rotations**\n\nI'm now clarifying the rotation rules based on additional observations. I've formulated that if moving down turns right, then the update to the ray is dr' = -dc, dc' = dr, a 90-degree counter-clockwise rotation. This is based on (0, 1) turning to (-1, 0). However, I've observed that \"6\" induces a clockwise 90-degree turn when a ray moves right.\n\n\n**Confirming Reflection Behavior**\n\nI've just confirmed the reflection behaviors with Example 5, and the behavior appears consistent. It seems that \"6\" consistently induces a clockwise 90-degree turn, while \"8\" induces a counter-clockwise 90-degree turn. This appears to be a consistent pattern, and I'm very confident now.\n\n\n",
